Researchers have developed algorithms to create uniform spanning trees with varying probabilities attached to each root node. These trees are generated through a sampling process, often using either a backtracking or dynamic programming approach, which, however, exhibit poor performance in terms of efficiency. We introduce a hybrid method combining elements of both schemes and present a thorough analysis demonstrating that this hybrid model yields substantially improved runtime complexities. Specifically, our hybrid model achieves optimal runtime complexities for trees and general graphs, respectively $O(m^{ 
m})$ and $O(2^{m}
m)^{ 
m}$, highlighting a notable increase in performance compared to the original algorithms.
